mail daily mail  always gets removed  and we give it a negative weight and so  now  we're right back where we started and  the cycle continues  um this is one example but really it  ended up happening for all sorts of  different features  because our training data and our test  data are inherently different  we can't totally fix this problem we  ended up alleviating it by removing  raw publisher features and other very  specific features like them  however because we still have features  based on interest scores  if a particular user clicks on the daily  mail a lot we will still learn to show  that particular user daily mail articles  so this one was fun statistical bleeding  so usually we have multiple rankers  running at the same time for comparison  one common situation where this is the  case is when we have an existing ranker  and we want to test out a new one to  potentially replace it  and when we do this we'll slowly ramp up  the fraction of users that get the new  ranker over the course of a week or two  for the sake of simplicity let's assume  that one ranker is all about exploration  represented here by the painting of  lewis and clark it's got a decent  likelihood of showing users brand new  articles out of no activity  hoping to find undiscovered gems in this  scenario the second ranker  is all about exploitation represented by  the businessman who's eavesdropping on  lewis and clark  the exploiter keeps showing users the  documents that have a lot of positive  interactions already  figuring they are good articles and  people will probably like them  you guys see where this is going  if you have 90 of users on the explore  ranker  and 10 on the exploit ranker the  explorer ranker will unearth all of the  good articles  and only then will the exploit ranker  take these documents under consideration  to show people  i've drawn an example up here to show  how the information gets passed between  the two  rankers you can see that when a new  article comes in  the exploiter won't show it to anyone  because it has no activity  but the explorer will and let's say that  this new article is a good one  and so the user clicks on it this causes  the statistics on the article to get  updated  which the exploiter also has access to  because the exploit ranker can see these  statistics it knows that it is a good  article and so it will show it to other  users  the exploit ranker will probably do  pretty good in this scenario  since it's making the explorer anchor do  all of the hard work  if the explorer anchor was the old  ranker and the exploiter was the new one  that we were considering switching over  to  the exploit one will be looking pretty  good right now and we'd be cranking up  the fraction of users who are on it  but what happens well eventually the  exploiter is the only ranker  and there is no one to do the  exploration and so the exploit ranker  will just keep showing users the same  sad couple documents  over and over again and never get any  better  based on how strongly weighted the  activity and the social features are  this is an exaggerated version of what  really happens  and so we have to be mindful to keep  retraining as we crank up the number of  users who are switched over  so the model learns over the correct  inputs  all right i saved the best one for last  how many of you know what simpson's  paradoxes  any a few so this is the wikipedia  example that i found to be most  intuitive  in the 1970s uc berkeley was sued for  bias against women who had a plot who  had applied for admission to graduate  school there  the admissions figures for fall of 1973  shown here  showed that men were except were  applying were much much more likely to  be accepted than women  and the difference was so large that it  was unlikely to be due to chance  but when examining the individual  departments  it appeared that no department was  significantly biased against women  in fact most departments had a small but  statistically significant bias  in favor of women the data from the six  largest departments are shown here  so what's the explanation  what happened was that women tended to  apply to competitive departments with  low rates of admission  even among qualified applicants whereas  men tended to apply to less competitive  departments  with high rates of admission among  qualified applicants  and so in summary it looked like the  women were being discriminated against  but in reality the different departments  did not have the same acceptance rates  and women tended to apply to the  departments that accepted fewer  applicants  the same thing happened to us we were  super confused at first  when we were looking at live results we  often break it down like this we'll be  comparing two rankers  and we'll look at both summary  information as well as how each ranker  did on each of our two platforms  iphone and web one day we were looking  at results  and we realized that ranker one did  better in summary but ranker two did  better on both of the individual  platforms  so we were super confused but my  colleague jason was the first to propose  that it might be an example of simpson's  paradox  and he was right what happened  was that we would shift the percentage  of users who got each ranker  and because users are more likely to use  the iphone at some times of day  and more likely to use the web at others  it ended up that more iphone users had  sessions with ranker one  while more web users had sessions with  ranker 2.  this combined with the fact that overall  people are more likely to click on  articles when they're on the iphone than  when they are on the web  and so because iphone users click more  and more of them got ranker 1  this corrupted the summary statistics to  make it look like ranker 1 was better  when in fact ranker 2 was better  after this we started resetting data  collection every time we changed ranker  percentages  so onto some results so  i never really explained to you what our  original ranking model looked like  before we switched to our machine  learning model  i'm not going to go into much detail  about it now but basically  some little small parts of it were  learned but mostly it was just a  hand-tuned model over a small number of  features  and so here you can see how the current  live ranker that we have up on  production right now  performed compared to the original  ranker users are sharing about 50  more um there's a 25 higher likelihood  that a user will click on the first  document  so even with presentation bias that's  still clearly a huge win  users are staying longer sessions are  about 15 percent longer  and lastly we have 15 percent fewer  total crap sessions um which means a  session where user doesn't click on  anything  share on anything do anything just kind  of scroll and peace out  and so overall we're super happy with  these results  and so here's a little summary of  everything that i've told you so far so  we collect information about our users  preferences and disk preferences  both explicit which is the favorites  that they've added and the people that  they follow  as well as implicit which is what stuff  do they actually click on  we analyze all of the articles in our  index to understand what they're about  so this is the topics and the publishers  and the key informative terms and the  date and the article  all of that and then we snapshot the  user sessions that we're actually going  to do  the direct learning on from those  sessions  we extract features which are meant to  be hints that should help us decide if a  user will like an article or not we then  learn weights for these features  which determine how the how the features  trade off between each other and how we  learn which features are more valuable  which features are less valuable  and then we use these weights to rank  articles for our user  and then lastly i want to thank the rest  of the prismatic back-end team  relevance at prismatic is not a  one-woman show the entire back end team  worked together on our relevance model  jason wolfe is here in the audience and  that strange leap you i should go try to  talk to him later and the rest of the  team is dave goland arya higgigi  and ian wong and that's it  thanks for listening and i think we got  about 10 minutes for questions
 

